const control=document.createElement('fieldset');control.id='preparationLearning';control.innerHTML='<legend>Learn success chances from experience</legend><label><input type="checkbox" id="prepLearnEnabled" style="width:auto"> Use checked preparation-based probabilities</label><p>No guessed probabilities are needed once a checked model is available. Match the same decision type and reusable success definition; record a deadline in your notes. The model learns from actual preparation time, including recovery, in three bands: 0–15, over 15–60, and over 60 minutes. It predicts the band of your planned preparation.</p><p id="prepModelStatus" role="status"></p><div id="prepModelEvidence" class="math-table"></div><details><summary>Probability learning formula and checks</summary><p>For a matching preparation-time band with s successes in n completed attempts, p = (s + 1)/(n + 2), the Beta(1,1) posterior predictive mean. The pooled baseline ignores preparation time and uses the same formula across all matching attempts. The prior and time bands are product choices.</p><p>At least 12 completed outcomes, three outcomes in each of two time bands, and four chronological checks are required. At each check, at least six outcomes must have been known before that decision was saved, with two in its planned-time band. Mean Brier error must beat the pooled baseline by more than 0.005. A future prediction also needs three outcomes in its band and planned minutes within a factor of two of that band’s observed (1 + minutes) range. Latest 200 outcomes per group. These thresholds are app conventions.</p><p>The displayed probability range is the posterior mean ± two posterior standard deviations, clipped to 0–100%; it is an approximate model-based range, not guaranteed coverage. Repeated attempts must be sufficiently comparable; changing difficulty, selection and dependence can invalidate the estimates. Better prediction does not establish a causal benefit of preparation.</p><p>The main score adds expected outcome value as one explicit criterion: [value(other) + p × (value(success) − value(other))]/10. Personal values use 0–100; the criterion uses 0–10 to share WAM’s arithmetic. Do not count the same outcome again in feelings or other benefits. Preparation time, spending and burden must already be included in the main resource fields; they are not added a second time.</p><p><a href="https://bookdown.org/kevin_davisross/stat415-handouts/beta-binomial.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Beta–Binomial model</a> · <a href="https://otexts.com/fpp3/tscv.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chronological forecast evaluation</a>.</p></details>';
